# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-
BLOCKS = {
  "base": [
    ("A Shed Pad Still Needs Real Subgrade Prep, Not Just a Tamped Spot",
     "<p>A shed footprint is small compared to a driveway or garage slab, but the ground underneath it is often the same flatwoods soil, Myakka, Smyrna or EauGallie among the common series, with a water table that can sit within 18 inches of the surface for part of most years. Treating a {city} shed pad as too small to need real subgrade work, removing saturated native soil and bringing in compacted fill, is how a light structure ends up with a door that no longer closes square a year or two after the pad settles unevenly. The weight on a shed pad is lower than on a driveway, but the soil underneath does not know the difference.</p>"),
    ("Ridge Sand Needs the Same Wetting for a Garage Slab as a Driveway",
     "<p>On the sand ridge soils west of Orlando, Candler and Astatula among them, the water table can sit more than 80 inches down, and that loose, excessively drained sand will not lock together under a compactor the way a firmer soil would without being wetted first. A detached garage or carport slab in {city} built on ridge sand needs the same shallow-lift compaction a driveway would get, even though the pad itself might look like a simpler pour. Skipping that step on a garage floor that will carry a vehicle's full weight, parked rather than rolling, risks a slow settle right where the tires sit longest.</p>"),
    ("A Vapor Retarder Isn't Automatic Under an Unheated Shed",
     "<p>Florida's residential code calls for a 6-mil polyethylene vapor retarder under most slabs on the ground, lapped at least 6 inches at the seams, but the code carves out exceptions for garages and unheated accessory structures. A simple storage shed pad in {city} that will never be conditioned space can often skip the retarder under that exception, while a garage addition meant to become finished, heated living area later does not get the same pass. Knowing which category a structure falls into before the pour, not after, is worth confirming with whoever pulls the permit, since adding a retarder once the slab is poured is not something that can be fixed later.</p>"),
    ("Grading a Pad to Shed Water Away From What Sits on It",
     "<p>A shed or garage pad that is flat or, worse, slightly bowl-shaped holds rainwater against its own edges instead of shedding it, and standing water working against a compacted base over a season does the same slow damage to a small pad that it would to a driveway, just with less margin because the pad is smaller to begin with. On a {city} lot, grading the pad to slope gently away from whatever sits on it, the shed itself, stored equipment, a boat or RV parked beside it, matters even on a pour this size. Flatwoods soils that already hold a shallow water table for months at a time make this step harder to skip without consequences.</p>"),
    ("Checking a Shed Pad's Elevation Against the House Before It's Poured",
     "<p>A detached shed or garage pad that ends up lower than the main house's finished floor can become the low point water heads for during a heavy storm, even if the pad itself is perfectly flat and well compacted. Before forming a {city} pad, checking its planned elevation against the house slab and the yard's overall grade catches this kind of problem while it is still cheap to fix, rather than after the first summer storm sends runoff pooling against a structure that was never meant to collect it. This matters most on flat flatwoods lots, where small elevation differences decide where water goes more than slope does anywhere else.</p>"),
  ],
  "build": [
    ("What Sits on the Pad Decides How Thick It Needs to Be",
     "<p>Florida's code minimum for a slab on the ground is 3 1/2 inches, but that number is written for ordinary floors, not for what actually ends up parked or stored on an accessory pad. A simple shed floor can often stay close to the standard 4-inch, 3,000 to 4,000 psi slab built for light, evenly spread loads, while a pad meant for an RV or a boat trailer in {city} needs to go to 5 or 6 inches, the same jump a driveway would make for a point load concentrated on a narrow set of wheels. Deciding which category a pad falls into before the pour avoids thickening it later, which means tearing it out.</p>"),
    ("Garage Floors and Carports Carry Their Own Strength Number by Code",
     "<p>Florida's residential code sets concrete strength for porches, carports and garage floors by weathering exposure rather than by a single flat number: 2,500 psi for negligible weathering, 3,000 for moderate and 3,500 for severe, a separate table from the strength used for an ordinary interior slab. A {city} garage addition or carport pad gets specified against that table rather than against whatever mix a driveway nearby happens to use, since the two surfaces answer to different code sections even when they sit a few feet apart. Mixing up which psi number applies to which structure is an easy paperwork mistake with a real cost difference once the order goes in to the plant.</p>"),
    ("A Slab Carrying a Stud Wall Needs a Thicker Edge Than One That Doesn't",
     "<p>A freestanding shed pad with no wall bearing directly on the concrete can stay a uniform thickness edge to edge, but a garage addition or carport slab that will carry a stud wall along its perimeter is a different load case, and we thicken that edge rather than keeping the same 4 inches all the way out to the form. That thickened perimeter spreads the wall's load over more concrete instead of concentrating it on a slab edge built for foot traffic and parked cars. On a {city} addition tying into the house, getting this detail into the forming plan before the pour matters more than it does on a pad that will only ever hold a shed.</p>"),
    ("Reinforcement Placed Where the Load Actually Sits",
     "<p>A slab built for general storage or light foot traffic gets the same baseline reinforcement, wire mesh or dispersed synthetic fiber, as any other flatwork, placed through the slab to hold a crack together rather than to stop one from forming. A pad built specifically for a boat trailer or an RV in {city} sees its heaviest, most repetitive load along two narrow wheel paths rather than spread evenly, worth flagging to whoever is designing the reinforcement and the thickness together, since a slab reinforced for even loading does not automatically perform the same way under two tire lines carrying most of the weight. The mesh or fiber choice works the same either way; what changes is where that reinforcement matters most.</p>"),
    ("A Small Pad Sometimes Needs Fewer Joints Than People Expect",
     "<p>Control joint spacing runs roughly 24 to 36 times a slab's thickness, which works out to around 8 to 12 feet on a standard 4-inch pour, so a 10-by-10 or 10-by-12 shed pad in {city} can sometimes get by with joints only at the perimeter, since the whole slab already falls inside that spacing without an interior cut. A 24-by-24 garage floor or a longer RV pad is a different story and needs interior joints laid out the same way a driveway would, square panels rather than long strips. Sizing the joint plan to the actual pad, instead of defaulting to the same pattern on every pour, is a detail worth asking about before forming starts.</p>"),
  ],
  "process": [
    ("Getting the Truck to a Pad That Isn't Next to the Street",
     "<p>A driveway sits right where a ready-mix truck can back up to it, but a shed pad, a carport slab tucked beside the house or an RV pad set back in the yard often does not, especially once a fence, a gate or a narrow side yard is in the way. On a {city} job where the truck cannot reach the forms directly, the pour goes by pump truck or by wheelbarrow relay from wherever the truck can park, which changes both the schedule and the crew size needed for the day. Confirming access before the pour date is set, not on pour morning, is what keeps a tight-access pad from turning into a delay nobody planned for.</p>"),
    ("Squaring a Shed or Garage Pad to the House Before Forms Go Up",
     "<p>Unlike a driveway, which mostly follows the curb and the garage opening it already has to line up with, a detached shed or garage addition pad usually gets laid out from scratch, staked and squared against the existing house corners rather than against anything already built on the lot. On a {city} job, that survey and layout step happens before grading starts, since an out-of-square form is much harder to correct once compacted fill and forms are both in place. Getting the layout right also decides how much the finished pad's edges line up with a fence line, a property setback or wherever the structure itself will eventually sit.</p>"),
    ("Why the Shed Can't Go Up the Same Week as the Pour",
     "<p>Curing has to continue for at least 3 days after finishing, whether through a curing compound or wet curing, before a slab has gained enough strength to be loaded, and a new shed, carport frame or stored equipment counts as a load the same as foot traffic would. On a {city} pad, that curing window has to be built into the schedule for whoever is putting up the structure, not treated as a formality to work around. Rushing a shed kit onto a pad that has not finished curing risks denting or marking a surface that is still gaining strength, even if it looks and feels solid within a day or two of the pour.</p>"),
    ("The Paperwork Step Looks Different on a Pad Under the HB 803 Threshold",
     "<p>Since July 1, 2026, Florida law exempts single-family homeowners and their contractors from a building permit for work valued under 7,500 dollars, as long as it is not structural work and the property is not in a flood hazard area, which covers a fair number of straightforward shed or garage pads. That exemption still requires filing a written request, so a {city} pad under the threshold skips the permit review itself but not the paperwork trail behind it. A pricier garage addition slab tied into the house, or any pad over that figure, goes through the full permit and inspection process instead, worth confirming before assuming a small pour qualifies for the lighter path.</p>"),
    ("Clearing an Old Pad in a Tight Backyard Takes Longer Than It Looks",
     "<p>Replacing an old, cracked shed or carport slab starts with breaking it up and hauling the pieces out, and in a backyard without direct truck access that debris often has to be carried or wheeled out the same route the new concrete will eventually come in through. On a {city} job like this, demolition day usually takes longer than the pour itself, not because the slab is large but because every piece of broken concrete has to travel through the same narrow gate or side yard. Once the old pad is cleared, the subgrade underneath finally becomes visible, which sometimes changes the compaction plan if the soil beneath a decades-old pour turns out softer than expected.</p>"),
  ],
  "risks": [
    ("A Garage Addition Slab Isn't the Same Permit Case as a Shed Pad",
     "<p>Structural concrete, the kind that becomes part of a building rather than sitting under a freestanding accessory structure, falls under Florida's state-certified structural masonry specialty contractor scope, which covers foundations, slabs, footers and walls. A detached shed pad in {city} and a slab poured as part of a garage addition tied into the house can look similar from a distance, but the addition slab is far more likely to need a permit and sign-off that a simple accessory pad would not. Assuming both pours qualify for the same lighter paperwork path because they are both just a slab is how a structural job ends up started without the review it actually needed.</p>"),
    ("Skipping the Vapor Retarder Now Can Cost More if the Shed Becomes a Room",
     "<p>Code allows a vapor retarder to be skipped under garages and unheated accessory structures, which covers most ordinary storage sheds, but that exception assumes the space stays unconditioned. A {city} shed built today without a retarder and converted into a workshop, an office or any heated, finished space later inherits a slab that was never built to block ground moisture from migrating up through it. Retrofitting a retarder under an existing slab is not realistic, so the only real fix at that point is a sealer or coating applied on top, a weaker solution than the retarder would have been if the pad's eventual use had been planned for at the pour.</p>"),
    ("A Shed-Grade Pad Cracks Fast Under an RV or Boat Trailer",
     "<p>A 4-inch pad built for a shed's distributed, lightweight storage load is not the same slab a loaded boat trailer or an RV needs, since both concentrate their weight onto a narrow set of wheels and, often, a tongue jack bearing on one small point for months at a time. Parking something that heavy on a {city} pad never built for point loads tends to crack first right along the wheel path or under the jack, well before the rest of the slab shows any wear. Telling a contractor what will actually sit on a pad before it is poured is a cheaper step than thickening or repairing it once a crack has started.</p>"),
    ("A Pad Graded Flat Instead of Sloped Sends Water Where It Shouldn't Go",
     "<p>A shed or garage pad poured level with, or slightly below, the surrounding grade collects runoff against its own edge instead of shedding it, and in coastal stretches of Sarasota and Manatee that carry FEMA flood zone designations, that same low spot can hold water even longer during a heavy storm. Standing water against a {city} pad's edge works into the compacted base the same slow way it would under a driveway, and against a structure sitting directly on the slab, it also means moisture reaching whatever is stored or built there sooner than it would on higher ground. Checking the pad's elevation against the surrounding yard before the pour catches this while it is still an easy fix.</p>"),
    ("The HB 803 Exemption Doesn't Cover Every Pad Under the Dollar Threshold",
     "<p>Florida's exemption from a building permit for work under 7,500 dollars, in effect since July 1, 2026, excludes structural work and flood-hazard-area properties no matter how small the job's price tag is, and a garage addition slab that ties into the house's structure is exactly the kind of work the exemption was not written to cover. A {city} homeowner who assumes a small pour is automatically exempt because of its cost, without checking whether it counts as structural or sits in a flood zone, risks a stop-work order partway through the job. The exemption also still requires a written request on file, so skipping that step is its own separate risk.</p>"),
  ],
  "care": [
    ("Re-Checking a Pad's Drainage Path Each Time the Yard Changes",
     "<p>A shed or garage pad's original grading can quietly stop working once a few years of landscaping, new mulch beds or settling soil change how water moves across a {city} yard, sometimes redirecting runoff back toward the pad instead of away from it the way it was built to drain. On flatwoods lots, where the water table already sits close to the surface for months at a stretch, that kind of slow change matters more than it would on better-draining ground. Walking the perimeter of the pad after any major landscaping work and confirming water still sheds away from it, rather than assuming the original grading still holds, is cheap insurance against the saturation that leads to base problems.</p>"),
    ("A Crack Under the Wheel Line Means Something Different Than One Elsewhere",
     "<p>On a pad that regularly carries a boat trailer or an RV, a crack that lines up with where the wheels or the tongue jack sit is worth more attention than ordinary shrinkage cracking running along a control joint somewhere else on the same {city} pad, since the first is tied to a repeated point load and the second usually is not. Noticing where on the slab a crack actually sits, not just that one exists, helps tell a cosmetic shrinkage crack from an early sign the pad is undersized for what is parked on it. Catching the second kind early is what makes repair, rather than replacement, still an option.</p>"),
    ("Signs That an Unconditioned Shed's Slab Is Letting Moisture Through",
     "<p>A shed pad built under the vapor retarder exception for unheated accessory structures is fine for ordinary storage, but it is worth watching for a musty smell, dampness along the base of stored boxes or cardboard that warps from the bottom up, especially after a wet season on a {city} lot with a shallow water table underneath. Those signs point to ground moisture moving up through a slab that was never meant to block it, not a leak somewhere else in the structure. Keeping stored items up on shelving or pallets rather than directly on the slab is a simple way to reduce the damage moisture coming through the concrete can do.</p>"),
    ("Why Stored Equipment Hides a Pad's Joints From View",
     "<p>A garage or shed pad used for storage tends to collect boxes, tools and yard equipment right along its edges, which is exactly where control joints and the pad's own perimeter sit, so leaves, mulch and dirt pack into those joints without anyone noticing for a season or two. Clearing a path along the edges of a {city} pad before the rainy months, even just enough to see the joints, lets water move the way the joints were cut to let it, instead of pooling behind whatever has been stacked against the slab's perimeter. It is easy upkeep to forget, specifically because the clutter that causes the problem is doing its job of keeping things organized everywhere else.</p>"),
    ("Aiming Irrigation Away From Equipment Parked on a Slab Edge",
     "<p>Watering schedules differ across the service area, year-round limits on the Orlando side and a stricter once-a-week schedule currently in place for Sarasota and Manatee, but either set of rules still allows a sprinkler head to drift onto a pad's edge over time regardless of how often it runs. A head aimed at a {city} pad where a boat, RV or trailer sits for long stretches keeps one edge of the slab wetter than the rest, which encourages mineral staining and, underneath whatever is parked there, slower drying after every cycle. Checking head aim against what is actually parked on the pad, not just the landscaping nearby, is worth doing once a season.</p>"),
  ],
}
